<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Zurayid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1083 CE – 1174 CE (approx.) · capital Aden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Isma'ili dynasty that ruled the port of Aden and grew rich on Indian Ocean trade between the Sulayhid and Ayyubid eras.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Zuray, a branch of the Yam tribe of Hamdan, were appointed governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sulayhid-dynasty">Sulayhid dynasty</a> around 1083, when the brothers al-Abbas and al-Mas'ud ibn Karam shared the city. As Sulayhid power faded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwa-al-sulayhi">Queen Arwa</a> they became independent rulers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Zurayids profited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden's</a> role as the main port between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; the documents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza">Cairo Geniza</a> describe Jewish and Muslim merchants trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, textiles and metals through the harbour. They followed the Hafizi branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a>, recognised by the Fatimid court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saba-ibn-abi-al-suud">Saba ibn Abi al-Su'ud</a> consolidated their rule in the 1130s and they extended inland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibla">Dhu Jibla</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1174 the Ayyubid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turan-shah-ibn-ayyub">Turan-Shah</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, ending the dynasty. Aden remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen's</a> chief port under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ayyubid-yemen">Ayyubid Yemen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rasulid-dynasty">Rasulid dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zurayid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zurayid dynasty" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Tiwahi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemen_1160_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1083 CE – 1174 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Abbas ibn Karam, Saba ibn Abi al-Su'ud, Muhammad ibn Saba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Isma'ili Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zurayids, Banu Zuray</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1128&amp;focus=zurayid-dynasty" aria-label="Zurayid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5916 1701 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zurayid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6236 1957L6207 1863M6236 1957L5811 1337M6236 1957L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6207 1863h0M5811 1337h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6236 1957h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aden. <a href="/atlas?year=1128&amp;focus=zurayid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

